.TH AUTHCHK 1
.SH NAME
authchk \- run keyring authentication and say whether it fits
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B authchk
.br
.B authchk fd
.I n
.RI [ ground ]
.br
.B authchk lora
.BR ground | satellite
.RB [ noack ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Authchk
runs
.IR keyring (2)'s
.B auth
between two certified identities and reports what it cost.
.PP
With no argument both ends run in one kernel over a
.IR pipe (2),
which is the form that answers the question everything else rests on:
whether the protocol runs at all on the target core.
.B Auth
is a Diffie-Hellman exchange and a signature check over 1024-bit
numbers on a board whose whole heap is measured in tens of kilobytes,
and no argument settles that.  If it does not fit over a pipe it will
not fit over a radio, and the radio would have been the expensive way
to find out.
.PP
With
.B fd
.I n
it runs one end over an already-open file descriptor, which is how it
reaches a link.  The identity used is the satellite's unless
.B ground
follows.
.PP
.B Lora
runs one end over a
.I lora (3)
conversation on
.BR /dev/radio0 ,
which is the same exchange with a radio underneath.  Both ends
.B connect
and neither announces, and that is not a detail: the exchange is
symmetric, each end sending before either has received, so an
announced conversation \- which has nobody to send to until it has
been spoken to \- cannot be one end of it.
.PP
Measured, two
.B mps2-an386
over a socket pair: 12 frames each way, unacknowledged, no retries,
and each end learning the other's identity.  With
.B ack
on it takes 28 frames and also works; the acknowledgement is not
needed, and
.B noack
is how that was found out.
.PP
The identities come from
.B /keys/ground
and
.BR /keys/satellite ,
made by
.IR mkradiokeys (1)
on a host and built into the boot image.  The signer's own key is not
on the board and must never be: a satellite that could mint identities
is a satellite that can be made to trust anything.
.PP
.IR Keyring (2)'s
.B auth
returns the identity the other end proved, or, when it proved nothing,
the reason.  The two are told apart by the secret and not by the
string:
.B libinterp/keyring.c
puts the peer's name in the first element and the shared secret in the
second on success, and leaves the second nil otherwise.  Reading the
first as an error reports
.B satellite
as a failure, which is what this program did until it was run.
.SH EXAMPLE
On
.BR mps2-an386 :
.PP
.EX
	; authchk
	ground-key-read ok
	satellite-key-read ok
	ground-side ok
	satellite-side ok
	same-length ok
	ground-learned-satellite ok
	satellite-learned-ground ok
	auth took 910 ms
	main in use 90176 before, 97152 after
	7 checks, 0 failed
	AUTHCHK-OK
.EE
.PP
910 milliseconds and 6976 bytes on a Cortex-M4, and each end learned
which certified identity the other holds without being told.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/authchk.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.I mkradiokeys (1),
.I radiossl (1),
.I lora (3)
.SH BUGS
The two identities are signed by a signer whose key is not in the
repository, but the identities themselves are, so they are secret from
nobody.  What is demonstrated is that the protocol runs and what it
costs, not that these particular keys are worth anything.
.PP
Both ends in one kernel share a pool, so the figure for memory is the
cost of two ends and not of one.
.PP
Nothing checks that an authentication message fits in a packet.
.IR Keyring (2)
writes a five-byte length and then the message, and
.B libinterp/keyring.c 's
.B nreadn
reads until it has the whole of it \- which on a message-delimited
device works only while each message is one packet.  Measured over
.I lora (3)
at 251 bytes of payload, the exchange is six messages each way and
none of them came near that; a larger key, or an owner name long
enough to matter, would not fit, and what happens then is the entry
below.
.PP
.B Announce
makes the conversation announce rather than connect, which cannot
work and is here to check that it says so: it reports
.B "input or format error"
at once, where it used to hang with nothing printed.  See
.IR keyring (2).
.PP
.B Setid
is passed 0.  Changing the user id on the strength of the exchange is
what a server would do and is not tried here.
